xnu 0387: a ring command that reads a file back

The command channel could write a file (`wfile`) and not read one, which is
why a daemon's explanation of a failure was unreadable: launchd can be pointed
at a log file with `StandardErrorPath`, but nothing could bring the file back.
Writing to `/dev/console` is not the way out either - with `mondrianfb_noconsole=1`
the kernel console does not carry userland writes into the ring (measured: a
bluetoothd job pointed at /dev/console left nothing in the dump).

`rf PATH [OFFSET [LEN]]` reads LEN bytes (1000 by default, one datagram) at
OFFSET through the same root the writer uses and answers with them raw, so the
host can `> file` the result. The read side is `mondrian_root_read()` next to
`mondrian_root_write()`, `vn_open` plus `vn_rdwr(UIO_READ)`, with the residual
count turned into the number of bytes read.

Not a general file server: 1000 bytes per call, no directory, no write. It
exists to make a process that cannot log observable, and the first user is
`bluetoothd`.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c b/bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c
index 10d40b4..47c788a 100644
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_ufs.c
@@ -2245,6 +2245,35 @@ out:
  */
 int mondrian_root_write(const char *path, const void *data, unsigned len);
 
+/*
+ * The read side of `wfile`: a daemon's stderr can be pointed at a file, and
+ * this is what brings it back over the command channel ("rf" in
+ * mondrian_usb.c). Returns the bytes read, or a negative errno.
+ */
+int
+mondrian_root_read(const char *path, void *data, unsigned len, off_t off)
+{
+	vnode_t vp = NULLVP;
+	vfs_context_t ctx = vfs_context_kernel();
+	int resid = 0;
+	int error;
+
+	if (len == 0) {
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+	error = vnode_open(path, FREAD, 0, 0, &vp, ctx);
+	if (error == 0) {
+		error = vn_rdwr(UIO_READ, vp, (caddr_t)data, (int)len, off,
+		    UIO_SYSSPACE, IO_NODELOCKED, vfs_context_ucred(ctx), &resid,
+		    vfs_context_proc(ctx));
+		vnode_close(vp, FREAD, ctx);
+	}
+	if (error != 0) {
+		return -error;
+	}
+	return (int)len - resid;
+}
+
